Connect finite state models to a normalized wavefunction with explicit boundaries (@physics-quantum-wavefunction: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Introduce-composite-states-and-separate-entanglement-from-a-classical-correlation.lingua#r_002A260BF999C8071224251C96") { r_00F087864FC26AFF0916C22CA8
A two-state model retains selected degrees of freedom. A particle's position in a one-dimensional region needs a function ψ(x), not just two coordinates. In the elementary spatial model, |ψ(x)|² is a probability density and ∫_R|ψ(x)|²dx gives the probability of a region R. Normalization requires total integral one. In one dimension ψ has units length^(−1/2), so the density has units inverse length. A density value at one point is not the probability of that point. Reuse the chemistry state account and [Review continuous models estimation and the scope of coverage](../../inquiry/core/Review-continuous-models-estimation-and-the-scope-of-coverage.lingua#r_0092AAD67F9A97EFCB0180340A).

Consider the ideal infinite well 0<x<L with ψ(0)=ψ(L)=0. Its Hamiltonian inside the interval acts as Hψ=−(ℏ²/2m)ψ″ for m>0, and the boundary conditions are part of its domain. This idealizes confinement; it is not an assertion that any real wall has literally infinite potential. For an energy eigenfunction Hφ=Eφ, multiply by φ* and integrate by parts. The boundary term vanishes, giving E∫|φ|²dx=(ℏ²/2m)∫|φ′|²dx≥0. If E=0, φ′ vanishes, so the boundary conditions force φ=0; a normalized eigenfunction therefore has E>0.

Write k²=2mE/ℏ². The already developed second-order equation gives φ=A sin(kx)+B cos(kx). The boundary at zero sets B=0; the other gives kL=nπ with positive integer n. Normalization using ∫₀ᴸsin²(nπx/L)dx=L/2 gives φ_n=sqrt(2/L)sin(nπx/L) and E_n=ℏ²π²n²/(2mL²). Negative n changes only the overall sign, and n=0 gives no state. Product-to-sum identities show different positive n are orthogonal. These finite derivations do not prove completeness for arbitrary square-integrable data or every self-adjoint-domain theorem.

The state φ_n exp(−iE_nt/ℏ) has time-independent position density. A normalized superposition of φ₁ and φ₂ with equal initial coefficients has density (φ₁²+φ₂²)/2+φ₁φ₂ cos[(E₂−E₁)t/ℏ]. Its left-half probability is 1/2+[4/(3π)]cos[(E₂−E₁)t/ℏ], because ∫₀ᴸ/²φ₁φ₂dx=4/(3π). Derive the integral from product-to-sum. An equal classical mixture of the two energies lacks the cross term and has left-half probability one-half. Thus the finite phase/mixture distinction survives in a spatial model.

**Read:** [Quantum Mechanics — David Tong](../../engineering/materials-engineering/sources/Quantum-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_0GY6BHCRA9CR5YGBY1AT9DRZY0), section 2.1.2, alongside its normalization and measurement sections. This is a bounded source-supported well reconstruction extended by the original two-mode comparison. **Work:** verify both boundaries, dimensions, normalization, positivity and orthogonality, derive the left-half formula and distinguish a stationary state from a stationary particle. Explain why projecting to finitely many energy modes can lose accuracy or change operator relations. A finite matrix cannot exactly satisfy [X,P]=iℏI for nonzero ℏ: its commutator has trace zero while iℏI has trace iℏn. Continuous operator domains and approximation errors therefore cannot be silently replaced by a small matrix.

**Continue:** [Introduce composite states and separate entanglement from a classical correlation](Introduce-composite-states-and-separate-entanglement-from-a-classical-correlation.lingua#r_002A260BF999C8071224251C96).

